var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"projections/#Projecting-between-representations-of-the-distribution-function","page":"Projections","text":"A distribution function is stored here in one of three forms. This page describes projections and sampling operations between them.\n\nA ParticleDistribution is the Klimontovich measure f_p(v) = sum_alpha w_alpha delta(v - v_alpha). The particle integrators evolve this representation, and its moments are weighted sums over particles.\nA SplineDistribution holds the coefficients of f_s(v) = sum_I hatf_I varphi_I(v). It is what the collision operators differentiate, and it gives a continuous f_s where a particle measure has none.\nA GridDistribution holds the values f_ij at the nodes of an n_x times n_v phase-space grid. It is what the reduced tensors contract.\n\nThis page is the map of the directions between them. Every implemented direction is shown here as an executed example, so a call that drifts from the API fails this page's own build; every direction that no method implements is named as absent. A page that is silent about a direction reads as if that direction existed, which is worse than a page that says it does not.\n\nnote: This is not the nullspace projection\nProjecting a state between representations is what this page is about. Projecting out the kernel of a Poisson operator is a different question, it belongs to PoissonSolvers, and it is answered there.","title":"Projecting between representations of the distribution function"},{"category":"section","location":"projections/#Which-directions-exist","page":"Projections","text":"from → to status call\nparticles → potential rhs implemented projection!(potential, dist)\nparticles → spline implemented, VD == 1 and VD > 1 projection(velocities, dist, final_dist)\nfunction → spline implemented project_function(f, sdist)\nspline → moments implemented, as sums over particles Σ_α π(v_α) f_s(v_α), not integrals; \"energy\" is projection_second_moment projection_density, projection_momentum, projection_second_moment\nspline → particles a stub that raises an error projection!(sdist, pdist)\nparticles → grid, grid → particles absent: no method takes both types —\nspline → grid, grid → spline absent; a spline has no x-dependence, so these directions need a spatial factor or spatial reduction —\nfunction → grid by sampling, grid.values .= g.(grid.x) .* h.(grid.v') —\ngrid → moments implemented, rectangle rule velocity_moments(grid)","title":"Which directions exist"},{"category":"section","location":"projections/#Particles-potential-rhs","page":"Projections","text":"This is the step a Vlasov–Poisson step makes at every stage value: the particle weights are deposited onto the basis of the potential's right-hand side. Each particle contributes to the local_width(basis) basis functions that do not vanish at its position, and an argument on a periodic axis is reduced into the domain before evaluation, so the unwrapped position the state keeps needs no handling at the call site.\n\nprojection! is not exported, so the block brings it into scope explicitly.\n\nThe rest of the block is the worked example of test/projections/projections.jl: the same npart, the same seed, the same sampler and the same tolerance, so that the figure below and the numerical assertion there are about one and the same sample.\n\nusing CairoMakie\nusing PoissonSolvers\nusing Random\nusing SimpleSplines\nusing VlasovMethods\nusing VlasovMethods: projection!\n\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\")\n\nnpart = 1000000\nncells = 32\norder = 5\n\ndomain = (0.0, 1.0)\npbasis = PeriodicBasisSpline(domain, order, ncells)\npotential = Potential(pbasis)\n\n# The sample is drawn from the global RNG, so without this the tolerance below is asserted\n# against a different sample on every run.\nRandom.seed!(1234)\n\nμ = 0.0\nσ = 2.0\nf = x -> exp(-0.5 * (4π * x - μ - 2π)^2 / σ^2) * sqrt(π * σ^2) / sqrt(2)\n\n# Substituting 4πx - 2π = 2π(2x - 1) turns `f` into √(2π)·exp(-2π²(x - ½)²), the normal\n# density with mean 1/2 and standard deviation 1/(2π). Rejecting draws outside the domain\n# samples that normal conditioned on the domain. Its density differs from `f` by the\n# normalisation for the excluded tails; the comparison below includes that truncation error.\nxmean = 0.5\nxstd = 1 / 2π\nsamples = Vector{Float64}(undef, npart)\nfor i in eachindex(samples)\n    x = xmean + xstd * randn()\n    while !(domain[begin] < x < domain[end])\n        x = xmean + xstd * randn()\n    end\n    samples[i] = x\nend\n\ndist = ParticleDistribution(1, 1, npart)\ndist.particles.x .= samples'\ndist.particles.w .= (ones(npart) ./ npart)'\n\nprojection!(potential, dist)\n\n# The deposit is the load vector ∫ρφᵢ. Solving the mass-matrix system gives the density's\n# spline coefficients, rather than the load vector plotted as though it were a density.\nquadrature = SplineQuadrature(pbasis)\nρ = Spline(pbasis, mass_factorization(quadrature) \\ PoissonSolvers.rhs(potential))\n\nx = range(domain[begin], domain[end]; length = 501)\n# The assertion is taken on the test's own grid, where its tolerance belongs.\nxa = domain[begin]:0.1:domain[end]\n\n# Match the test's Euclidean norm and absolute tolerance on its grid. The error includes\n# sampling, truncation and spline approximation; no tolerance is widened for this page.\nerr = sqrt(sum(abs2, f.(xa) .- ρ.(xa)))\nerr ≤ 2.5e-2 || error(\"the projected density is off by \", err)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (760, 420))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"density\",\n    title = \"10⁶ particles on a periodic quartic basis\")\nhist!(ax, samples; bins = 100, weights = fill(1 / npart, npart),\n    normalization = :pdf, color = (:gray, 0.6), label = \"particles\")\nlines!(ax, x, ρ.(x); color = :red, linewidth = 2, label = \"projected ρ\")\nlines!(ax, x, f.(x); color = :black, linestyle = :dash, label = \"analytic f\")\n\naxislegend(ax)\n\n# Three curves and three plots: a figure that silently loses one of them is the failure this\n# assertion exists for.\nlength(ax.scene.plots) == 3 || error(\"expected three plots, got \", length(ax.scene.plots))\n\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe histogram shows the sampled, truncated density. The spline rho reconstructs the deposited particles, and f is the untruncated normal density. The assertion uses the same Euclidean norm and tolerance as test/projections/projections.jl.","title":"Particles → potential rhs"},{"category":"section","location":"projections/#Particles-spline","page":"Projections","text":"The projection of the Klimontovich measure onto a spline basis is the L^2 projection, i.e. the coefficients hatf_I = sum_K mathbbM^-1_IK sum_alpha  v_alpha in mathrmsupp varphi_K w_alpha varphi_K(v_alpha). Pairing a delta with a basis function is an evaluation, so the load vector is the same deposition as above, and the mass matrix is what differs between the two directions. There is one method for a one-dimensional velocity space and one for a VD-dimensional one; nothing else differs between them.\n\nvelocities is passed separately from the distribution because the implicit solvers evaluate the right-hand side at a stage value rather than at the particles' stored positions.\n\nusing Random\nusing SimpleSplines\nusing VlasovMethods\n\n# A seed of its own, so that the printed numbers below are the same on every build: the\n# deposition example keeps the seed of the test it ports.\nRandom.seed!(0xC0FFEE)\n\nnpart = 1000\nvelocities = randn(npart)\n\npdist = ParticleDistribution(1, 1, npart)\npdist.particles.v[1, :] .= velocities\npdist.particles.w[1, :] .= 1 / npart\n\nsdist = SplineDistribution(1, 1, 21, 4, (-10.0, 10.0), 0, Free())\n\nprojection(velocities, pdist, sdist)\nSpline(BSplineBasis, (23,))\n\nA particle that has left the velocity domain is an error rather than a warning: its weight would be dropped from f_s while it kept moving, and f_s would then be zero at its position, which makes the f_sf_s of every collision operator a division by zero. Enlarge the domain, or give the mesh oversized end cells with length_big_cell.","title":"Particles → spline"},{"category":"section","location":"projections/#Function-spline","page":"Projections","text":"A prescribed function takes the same two steps against the quadrature the basis already carries. project_function integrates f against varphi_I on the Gauß–Legendre nodes, project_Maxwellian is the one call for the case that most examples need, and both work in any number of velocity dimensions.\n\nh(v) = exp(-v^2 / 2) / sqrt(2π)\nfsdist = SplineDistribution(1, 1, 21, 4, (-10.0, 10.0), 0, Free())\n\nproject_function(h, fsdist)\nSpline(BSplineBasis, (23,))\n\nf is called with a scalar for a one-dimensional velocity space and with a VD-tuple otherwise, and SVector accepts both, so project_Maxwellian is one method for every dimension.","title":"Function → spline"},{"category":"section","location":"projections/#Spline-moments","page":"Projections","text":"These helper functions compute sums over the particles, sum_alpha pi(v_alpha) f_s(v_alpha), not integrals int pi(v) f_s(v)  dv. The spline is sampled only at the supplied velocities. The isDerivative keyword puts f_s in place of f_s, and no particle weight enters either. The collision operators do not call these helpers; the conservative Lenard–Bernstein coefficients use weighted particle moments instead.\n\nThe third of them is the second moment v^2 and is called projection_second_moment, not an energy: an energy would carry the factor tfrac12, and in a phase-space model it also carries the x-dependence that the grid's velocity_moments is written to keep.\n\n(density = VlasovMethods.projection_density(sdist, velocities),\n    momentum = VlasovMethods.projection_momentum(sdist, velocities),\n    second_moment = VlasovMethods.projection_second_moment(sdist, velocities))\n(density = 282.49524798511897, momentum = 2.133043176731971, second_moment = 132.1982912263524)\n\nHere sdist is the spline of the particles projected two sections above, and velocities is that same sample, so the three numbers are the sampled moments of the projected distribution.","title":"Spline → moments"},{"category":"section","location":"projections/#Spline-particles","page":"Projections","text":"This direction is not implemented. There is a method for it and it raises rather than converting:\n\nfunction projection!(init_dist::SplineDistribution, final_dist::ParticleDistribution)\n    error(\"projecting a spline distribution back onto particles is not implemented\")\nend\n\nTwo meanings are available for it, and neither is chosen yet:\n\nDraw a new particle sample from f_s — the reverse of the projection above, and the direction a reduced model would lift a reduced state back onto particles with.\nKeep the particle positions and set the weights w_alpha propto f_s(v_alpha) — the cheap one, and the one whose result is not a sample of f_s but a quadrature of it at the positions that happen to be there.\n\nEach needs a rule for what to do where f_s  0, which a projection of an undersampled particle measure produces readily and which K8 of KNOWN_ISSUES.md records as open. Choosing between the two is a modelling decision rather than an implementation detail, so this page names the direction as absent and KNOWN_ISSUES.md records it.","title":"Spline → particles"},{"category":"section","location":"projections/#Particles-grid,-and-spline-grid","page":"Projections","text":"These directions are absent. A GridDistribution exists and is a DistributionFunction beside the other two, but no method takes a ParticleDistribution and a GridDistribution together, or a SplineDistribution and a GridDistribution together.\n\nA SplineDistribution represents a function of v only. Mapping a general phase-space grid to one therefore needs a choice of spatial slice or spatial average, which these interfaces do not provide. In the other direction, a spatial factor g(x) can supply the missing dependence through the separable product g(x) cdot f_s(v).","title":"Particles ↔ grid, and spline ↔ grid"},{"category":"section","location":"projections/#Function-grid","page":"Projections","text":"A function of x and v becomes a grid by being sampled at the nodes, which is the one conversion here that needs no projection at all:\n\ng(x) = 1 + 0.3 * cos(x)\n\ngrid = GridDistribution(8, 161, (0.0, 2π), (-6.0, 6.0))\ngrid.values .= g.(grid.x) .* h.(grid.v')\ngrid\nGridDistribution{Float64}((8, 161))\n\nThe two axes are not symmetric, and the sampling above has to respect that: x is periodic and its right end is the node the left end already is, so it is not stored, while v is bounded and both of its ends are nodes.","title":"Function → grid"},{"category":"section","location":"projections/#Grid-moments","page":"Projections","text":"velocity_moments integrates the grid over v by the rectangle rule, int f(x_i v)  dv approx sum_j f_ij h_v, at each x-node:\n\nvelocity_moments(grid)\n(density = [1.2999999979829786, 1.2121320324752756, 0.9999999984484447, 0.7878679644216148, 0.6999999989139108, 0.7878679644216148, 0.9999999984484447, 1.2121320324752756], momentum = [-6.523685011967101e-17, -3.4184596630729374e-17, 6.056098878203994e-17, 1.8557802419301157e-17, -2.3925243824488827e-18, 1.8557802419301157e-17, 6.056098878203994e-17, -3.4184596630729374e-17], energy = [0.6499999612354588, 0.6060659810335651, 0.49999997018112224, 0.39393395932867925, 0.34999997912678543, 0.39393395932867925, 0.49999997018112224, 0.6060659810335651])\n\nThe rule is the integral of the bilinear interpolant plus tfrach_v2(f_i1 + f_i n_v), so it is exact up to the two end values and first order in h_v where those do not vanish. The moment functions above are its opposite number on the spline: those are sums over particles, and this is an integral over a grid.","title":"Grid → moments"},{"category":"section","location":"#VlasovMethods","page":"Home","text":"Documentation for VlasovMethods.\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.CollisionEntropy-Union{Tuple{}, Tuple{XD}} where XD","page":"Home","text":"(ent::CollisionEntropy)()\n\nThe discrete entropy\n\nS_h = int_Omega f_s  log f_s  dv \n\nevaluated on the Gauß-Legendre grid the spline basis already carries, in any number of velocity dimensions.\n\nnote: The sign is the manuscripts' `S`\nBoth manuscripts define S = -int f log f in the continuum and then report int f_s log f_s in their figures; this returns the latter, so it decreases monotonically. Read as \"entropy\" with the manuscripts' continuum sign it is -S, and a plot of it labelled \"entropy\" and expected to grow is inverted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.CollisionEntropy"},{"category":"type","location":"#VlasovMethods.GeometricIntegrator","page":"Home","text":"GeometricIntegrator(model::VlasovPoisson{1, 1, <:GridDistribution}, tspan, tstep,\n                    method = Integrators.ImplicitMidpoint())\n\nThe GeometricIntegrator that steps the grid 1D1V Vlasov–Poisson model model over tspan with step tstep, in the state vec(model.distribution.values).\n\nmethod is any GeometricIntegrators method for an ODEProblem. ImplicitMidpoint() keeps H; RK4() is the one scripts/landau_damping_1d1v.jl uses, because the implicit midpoint's dense Newton Jacobian is too large on that script's n_v. The scheme runs in Float32 and Float64; FFTW has no Float16 plan. The vector field is _grid_1d1v_rhs!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.GeometricIntegrator"},{"category":"type","location":"#VlasovMethods.GridDistribution","page":"Home","text":"GridDistribution{DT, XT, VT, FT}\n\nThe distribution function of a 1D1V phase space, stored as its values at the nodes of an nx × nv grid. It is a DistributionFunction{DT, 1, 1}, beside ParticleDistribution and SplineDistribution.\n\nThe grid\n\nx is periodic: x_i = x_a + (i-1) h_x for i = 1 dots n_x, with h_x = (x_b - x_a)  n_x. The node x_b is the node x_a again and is not stored.\nv is bounded: v_j = v_a + (j-1) h_v for j = 1 dots n_v, with h_v = (v_b - v_a)  (n_v - 1). Both ends of the range are nodes.\n\nvalues is the nx × nv matrix f_ij = f(x_i v_j). Its column-major linear order, x running fastest, is the order the grid stencils such as _apply_∫dv! use, so vec(dist.values) is their state vector.\n\nEvaluation\n\ndist(x, v) interpolates bilinearly between the four surrounding nodes. It is periodic in x and zero outside v_a v_b.\n\nMoments\n\nvelocity_moments integrates over v by the rectangle rule, int f(x_i v)  dv approx sum_j f_ij h_v. This is the integral of the interpolant plus tfrach_v2 (f_i1 + f_i n_v), so it is exact up to the values at the two ends of the velocity range and first order in h_v where those do not vanish.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.GridDistribution"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.GridDistribution-Tuple{Integer, Integer, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"GridDistribution(nx, nv, xdomain, vdomain)\n\nA GridDistribution of Float64 zeros on nx × nv nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.GridDistribution"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.GridDistribution-Union{Tuple{DT}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{DT}, Any, Any}} where DT","page":"Home","text":"GridDistribution(values::AbstractMatrix, xdomain, vdomain)\n\nWrap the node values values of size nx × nv on the periodic interval xdomain and the bounded interval vdomain. Each domain is anything with a first and a last.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.GridDistribution"},{"category":"type","location":"#VlasovMethods.PotentialReducedTensor","page":"Home","text":"PotentialReducedTensor(tensor::GridTensor{DT, 3}, Pi, Pj, Pk)\n\nThe lazy size(Pi, 2) × size(Pj, 2) × size(Pk, 2) array that projects the first two indices of the bracket tensor tensor onto the columns of Pi and Pj, and its third index onto the columns of Pk. With R = ReducedTensor(tensor, Pi, Pj),\n\nPotentialReducedTensor[i, j, k] = ∑_K R[i, j, K] * Pk[K, k] .\n\nPi, Pj and Pk are matrices with one row per grid node. The nodes are counted in the order of the grid's own linear indices, the first direction fastest, as in vec(dist.values) of a GridDistribution. The grid may have any dimension.\n\nThe constructor builds ReducedTensor(tensor, Pi, Pj) first, so the checks of that constructor run here as well: Pi and Pj need one row per node, or it throws a DimensionMismatch, their element type has to be DT, or it throws an ArgumentError, and every direction of the grid needs at least 2w + 1 nodes for the tensor's stencil width w, or it throws an ArgumentError. Pk needs one row per node, or the constructor throws a DimensionMismatch, and the element type DT, or it throws an ArgumentError. An index off the array throws a BoundsError.\n\nThe tensor's contract is ReducedTensor's and is assumed, not checked: tensor is a bracket's tensor on the grid that bracket was built on, nonzero only where its stencil says so. bracket_tensor builds such a tensor from an Arakawa. A tensor written by hand on another grid than its bracket breaks the contract silently.\n\ngetindex allocates nothing, and neither does materialising the array into a preallocated output.\n\njulia> using GeometricBrackets: Arakawa\n\njulia> gt = bracket_tensor(Arakawa(3, 3, 0.5, 0.5));\n\njulia> size(PotentialReducedTensor(gt, ones(9, 2), ones(9, 1), ones(9, 4)))\n(2, 1, 4)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.PotentialReducedTensor"},{"category":"type","location":"#VlasovMethods.Snapshots","page":"Home","text":"Snapshots{T, PS}\n\nThe snapshots of a parameter sweep: the positions X, the velocities V, the accelerations A, the potentials Φ, and the diagnostics W, K and M, on the sweep's ParameterSpace.\n\nX, V and A are indexed [component, particle, time, sample] and have the size (nd, nₚ, nₛ, nparam), with nparam = length(paramspace). X[:, :, t, p] is the (nd, nₚ) matrix of the positions saved at the t-th of the nₛ saved times of the sample paramspace(p), X[i, j, t, p] is component i of particle j, and V and A hold the velocities and the accelerations of that state the same way. So vec(X[:, :, t, p]) is the component-fastest order of vec(particles.x) of that state, and a snapshot matrix built from these arrays has one column per saved time and sample.\n\nΦ has the size (nh, nₛ, nparam): the potential is a scalar on the one dimension of the Poisson problem, so it carries no component axis, and nh is the number of degrees of freedom of the sweep's Poisson solver. W, K and M have the size (nₛ, nparam), one value each per saved time and sample. W is the field energy, not a particle weight: the snapshots store no weights.\n\nThe component count nd of X, V and A and the degree count nh of Φ are the leading extents of those arrays, so they are read from the arrays rather than stored beside them.\n\n== compares all eight fields, paramspace included.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.Snapshots"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.Snapshots-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, ReducedComplexityModeling.ParameterSpace}","page":"Home","text":"Snapshots(T, nd, nₚ, nh, nₛ, paramspace)\n\nThe zero Snapshots{T} of nd components, nₚ particles, nh potential degrees of freedom, nₛ saved times and length(paramspace) samples, which a sweep fills as it runs. nh = 0 is the sweep of a model with no potential, such as one of VlasovWithExternalFields, and its empty Φ round-trips through HDF5 like any other.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.Snapshots"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.Snapshots-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{ParticleMethods.ParticleList{T, ST} where ST<:AbstractMatrix{T}, PoissonSolvers.PoissonSolver{T}, Any, ReducedComplexityModeling.ParameterSpace}} where T","page":"Home","text":"Snapshots(particles::ParticleList, poisson::PoissonSolver, nₛ, paramspace)\n\nThe zero Snapshots{T} of a sweep of particles on the Poisson solver poisson, with nₛ saved times and length(paramspace) samples: nd = size(particles.x, 1) components, nₚ = length(particles) particles and nh = length(poisson) potential degrees of freedom. Both element types are T.\n\nparticles.v needs the nd rows of particles.x, because X, V and A share one component count and the symplectic structure of the reduced model pairs positions and velocities of one size. A list that holds another count throws an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.Snapshots"},{"category":"type","location":"#VlasovMethods.SplineDistribution","page":"Home","text":"SplineDistribution(xdim, vdim, nknots, s_order, domain, length_big_cell, bc = Free())\n\nBuild a SplineDistribution on nknots equally spaced breakpoints across domain, of spline order s_order, with boundary condition bc on every velocity axis.\n\ns_order is the order k = p+1, the same convention as SimpleSplines.order — so s_order = 4 is the cubic basis both collision-operator manuscripts specify, and s_order = 3 is quadratic.\n\nlength_big_cell > 0 appends one oversized cell of that width at each end of domain, outside it, giving a GeneralMesh. That is the device for keeping a particle that strays out of the resolved region inside the support of the basis; with 0 the mesh is uniform on domain exactly.\n\ndomain is anything with a first and a last — a two-tuple, a vector or a range.\n\nbc may be a SimpleSplines.BoundaryCondition, a two-tuple for the two ends, or one of the symbols :Dirichlet, :Periodic and :nothing that the drivers in scripts/ use, where :nothing means Free(). Any other symbol is an error. The default is Free() — the unconstrained clamped basis, which is the only choice that reproduces 1, v and v^2 and hence the only one on which the conservative schemes conserve.\n\nThe mass matrix is always the exact Galerkin one, mathbbM_ij = int varphi_i varphi_j; there is no lumped-mass option.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.SplineDistribution"},{"category":"type","location":"#VlasovMethods.SplineDistribution-2","page":"Home","text":"SplineDistribution{DT, XD, VD}\n\nThe distribution function represented as a spline in velocity, f_s(v) = sum_I hatf_I  varphi_I(v), together with everything needed to project onto it and to integrate against it.\n\nVD is the number of velocity dimensions. For VD == 1 the basis is a one-dimensional SimpleSplines B-spline basis and the coefficients are a vector; for VD > 1 it is a TensorProductBasis and the coefficients are a VD-dimensional array. Nothing else about the two cases differs — the evaluation, the projection and the mass solve are the same calls.\n\nFields\n\nspline — the SimpleSplines.Spline, which shares coefficients. A projection writes that array, and every reference to the spline sees the new function without a rebuild.\nbasis — the spline basis.\nquadrature — the assembly table: the basis tabulated at the Gauß-Legendre points, the weights, and the mass operator.\ncoefficients — the degrees of freedom hatf.\n\nThe mass matrix is never formed as a Kronecker product\n\nFor VD > 1 the mass matrix is mathbbM^(VD) otimes dots otimes mathbbM^(1), and a solve is VD one-dimensional solves applied along each axis. That is an identity, not an approximation, and it is what makes the higher-dimensional cases tractable at all: forming the product and factorising it densely would mean a 1681×1681 matrix for the 41-element cubic basis of a two-dimensional velocity space, and 68921² in three dimensions, which does not fit.\n\nThe boundary condition is not a free choice\n\nThe L^2 projection reproduces int pi(v) f  dv for a polynomial pi exactly when pi lies in the span of the basis, so the mass, momentum and energy of f_s agree with those of the particles only if 1, v and v^2 are in the span. polynomial_reproduction(basis) reports the largest degree available: p for the clamped basis (Free()), 0 for a periodic one — v is not periodic — and -1 for a Dirichlet-recombined one, where not even the constants survive.\n\nMeasured on a cubic basis over -88 by scripts/verify_conservation.jl: Free() reproduces all three to 10^-14, Periodic() gets v wrong by 12 and v^2 by 012, and Dirichlet() gets even the constant wrong by 4 times 10^-2.\n\nnote: What this does and does not break\nIt does not by itself break the particle-level conservation of the conservative Lenard-Bernstein operator: its coefficients A_1 A_2 are solved from the two conservation constraints, which are conditions on particle sums, so sum_alpha w_alpha dotv_alpha and sum_alpha w_alpha v_alpha dotv_alpha vanish at round-off on any basis. What it breaks is the agreement between the particle and spline representations, and therefore every diagnostic computed from f_s — the entropy above all, which is what the H-theorem is a statement about. See check_conservation_basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.SplineDistribution"},{"category":"type","location":"#VlasovMethods.UniformField","page":"Home","text":"UniformField(E, B)\n\nA constant electric and magnetic field, E and B in Cartesian components, stored as SVector{3,T} with T the promoted element type of the two arguments. It is constant in x and in t, so it is the field of the analytic cases: a gyro-orbit at E = 0, and a constant acceleration along B at E ∥ B.\n\nE and B are two vectors of length three, or two SVector{3}s; a length other than three throws the DimensionMismatch of SVector{3}. A constant field of Int coordinates is written UniformField(SVector(0, 0, 0), SVector(0, 0, 0)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.UniformField"},{"category":"type","location":"#VlasovMethods.VlasovHamiltonian","page":"Home","text":"VlasovHamiltonian(model)\n\nThe energy of the Vlasov–Poisson model model, as an invariant h(t, q, params),\n\nh(t q w) = frac12sum_i w_i v_i^2 + frac12int (partial_xphi)^2 mathrmdx \n\nwhere phi solves the Poisson problem for a fresh deposit of the positions in q with the weights params.w. The call leaves that deposit in model.potential. Each acceleration substep deposits its own state before it reads the field, so this does not change a trajectory.\n\nThe field term is the Gauss quadrature of (partial_xphi)^2 on the basis of the potential, tabulated once by the constructor, so a call allocates nothing. Its terms are non-negative, so the sum keeps their relative accuracy also for a small field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.VlasovHamiltonian"},{"category":"type","location":"#VlasovMethods.VlasovPoisson","page":"Home","text":"VlasovPoisson(dist::DistributionFunction, potential::Potential)\n\nThe Vlasov–Poisson model of one species with the distribution dist, whose field is the solution of potential.\n\nFor a ParticleDistribution or a SplineDistribution, the field is the potential's solve for the deposit of the distribution, and potential is on a B-spline basis.\n\nFor a 1D1V GridDistribution, the field is phi = G rho with rho_i = h_v sum_j f_ij, and the constructor assembles the n_x times n_x Green's matrix G of the potential's backend once. GeometricIntegrator(model, tspan, tstep, method) steps the model. The constructor throws:\n\nArgumentError unless the potential's solver is a PoissonSolverFFT or a PoissonSolverMatrixFree;\nDimensionMismatch unless the potential has one degree of freedom per x-node of dist;\nArgumentError unless the potential's x-nodes are dist.x, and unless dist has at least three nodes in each direction.\n\nThe grid scheme is periodic in v: it is valid only while f vanishes to eps(T)·maximum(f) at both ends of the velocity range.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.VlasovPoisson"},{"category":"type","location":"#VlasovMethods.VlasovWithExternalFields","page":"Home","text":"VlasovWithExternalFields(dist, field; charge = one(T), mass = one(T))\n\nA VlasovModel of one particle species in an external electromagnetic field: the particles of dist, a ParticleDistribution{T,3,3}, pushed by field. charge and mass are the species' charge and mass, in units in which the equation of motion reads ẍ = (q/m)(E + ẋ × B), and both are stored as T. The constructor throws an ArgumentError unless the converted mass is positive. An empty particle list is a vacuum run and not an error: the model, its SplittingMethod and run! work over the empty state.\n\nThe field is any value that defines electric_field and magnetic_field for an SVector{3} coordinate; the model puts no bound on the type of its field parameter, so the two functions are the whole contract. UniformField is the field type this package ships, and the ElectromagneticFields extension adds the two methods for that package's FieldFunctions of a Cartesian chart — FieldFunctions(ABCEquilibrium(a, b, c)) is passed to the constructor as it is. In a curvilinear chart the components are not Cartesian, so the extension defines no method and the first kick raises its MethodError.\n\nThe method is SplittingMethod(model, tspan, tstep): a Strang splitting of the drift ẋ = v and the Lorentz force v̇ = (q/m)(E + v × B), with the rotational part of the force a Boris push. One step is a half drift, two half Boris pushes with the field evaluated at the step's start, and a half drift. GeometricIntegrators.integrate(method.integrator) and run!(method, h5file) serve it as they serve the Vlasov–Poisson model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.VlasovWithExternalFields"},{"category":"method","location":"#SimpleSplines.mass_matrix-Tuple{SplineDistribution}","page":"Home","text":"mass_matrix(dist::SplineDistribution)\n\nThe assembled mass matrix. For VD > 1 this forms the Kronecker product, which the representation exists to avoid; it is here for tests and diagnostics, not for solving.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimpleSplines.mass_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"#SimpleSplines.mass_operator-Tuple{SplineDistribution}","page":"Home","text":"mass_operator(dist::SplineDistribution)\n\nThe mass operator of the basis — a factorised or circulant one for VD == 1, a KroneckerMass for VD > 1. Solve with mass_solve!(out, mass_operator(dist), rhs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimpleSplines.mass_operator"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.CLB_rhs_GI!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractArray{ST}, Any}} where ST","page":"Home","text":"The conservative Lenard-Bernstein right-hand side,\n\ndotv_alpha = - nu left( fracf_s(v_alpha)f_s(v_alpha) + A_1 + A_2 v_alpha right) \n\nwhich is eq:velocity_ode of the Lenard-Bernstein manuscript with the opposite sign.\n\nnote: The sign here is the correct one and the manuscript's is not\nThe manuscript writes partial_t f = partial_v (a f) with a = nu (ff + A_1 + A_2 v) and then dotv_alpha = a(v_alpha). Continuity is partial_t f + partial_v (U f) = 0, so partial_t f = +partial_v(a f) forces U = -a and hence dotv = -a. The check: for a Maxwellian of width sigma  1 — too broad — a = nu v (1 - sigma^-2)  0 for v  0, so dot v = +a would push particles outward and widen an already too-wide distribution instead of relaxing it. Conservation is unaffected either way, the constraints being homogeneous, so no published result changes; but the ODE as typeset relaxes backwards in time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.CLB_rhs_GI!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.FullyReducedTensor-Union{Tuple{DT}, Tuple{MultiIndexArrays.GridTensor{DT, 3, MT} where MT<:(MultiIndexArrays.MultiIndexLazyArray{DT, 3, FT, AX} where {FT<:Union{Function, Type}, AX<:(Tuple{var\"#s5\", var\"#s5\", var\"#s5\"} where var\"#s5\"<:MultiIndexArrays.MultiIndexAxis)}), AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}} where DT","page":"Home","text":"FullyReducedTensor(tensor::GridTensor{DT, 3}, Pi, Pj, Px)\n\nThe lazy size(Pi, 2) × size(Pj, 2) × size(Px, 2) array that projects the third index of the bracket tensor tensor onto the columns of Px, read at the x index of the node. With R = ReducedTensor(tensor, Pi, Pj) and (n₁, n₂) the grid indices of the node K,\n\nFullyReducedTensor[i, j, k] = ∑_K R[i, j, K] * Px[n₁, k] .\n\nPx has one row per x node, in the order of dist.x, and not one per grid node. The result is the PotentialReducedTensor whose projection has the row Px[n₁, :] for each node.\n\nThe grid needs two dimensions, or the function throws an ArgumentError. Px needs nx rows, or it throws a DimensionMismatch, and the element type DT, or it throws an ArgumentError. PotentialReducedTensor gives the other checks, the node order, the tensor's contract and the allocations.\n\njulia> size(FullyReducedTensor(gt, ones(9, 2), ones(9, 1), ones(3, 4)))   # gt as above\n(2, 1, 4)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.FullyReducedTensor"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.LB_rhs!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{Any, AbstractArray{ST}, Any, Any}} where ST","page":"Home","text":"The plain Lenard-Bernstein right-hand side,\n\ndotv_alpha = - nu left( fracf_s(v_alpha)f_s(v_alpha) + v_alpha right) \n\ni.e. the conservative operator of eq:cons_LB with the coefficients fixed at A_1 = 0, A_2 = 1. It conserves mass but not momentum or energy — that is the whole point of the conservative variant.\n\nnote: The division by `f_s` was missing\nBoth right-hand sides computed -ν (f_s' + v f_s), which is the collisional flux Ff_s, not the advection coefficient. The two differ by a factor of f_s(v_alpha): the equilibrium condition f_sf_s = -v is the same either way, so the fixed point was right, but particles in the tails — where f_s is small — were barely advected and the whole transient was wrong.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.LB_rhs!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.MaxwellianDistribution-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"MaxwellianDistribution(v)\n\nThe normalised Maxwellian of unit temperature and zero mean in d = length(v) velocity dimensions,\n\nf_M(v) = (2pi)^-d2  expleft(-tfrac12 v^2right) \nqquad int_mathbbR^d f_M  dv = 1 \n\nnote: The normalisation is dimension-dependent\nIt was fixed at 1(2pi), which is the d = 2 value. That was correct where the function was used — project_Maxwellian existed only for a two-dimensional velocity space — but it is wrong for any other d, and silently so: in one dimension it is short by a factor sqrt2pi, so a \"normalised\" Maxwellian integrated to 0399 rather than to 1. The Lenard-Bernstein manuscript's f_M(v) = e^-v^22sqrt2pi is the d = 1 case of the expression above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.MaxwellianDistribution"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.VelocityReducedMatrix-Union{Tuple{DT}, Tuple{MultiIndexArrays.GridTensor{DT, 3, MT} where MT<:(MultiIndexArrays.MultiIndexLazyArray{DT, 3, FT, AX} where {FT<:Union{Function, Type}, AX<:(Tuple{var\"#s5\", var\"#s5\", var\"#s5\"} where var\"#s5\"<:MultiIndexArrays.MultiIndexAxis)}), AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}} where DT","page":"Home","text":"VelocityReducedMatrix(tensor::GridTensor{DT, 3}, Pi, Pj, v²)\n\nThe lazy size(Pi, 2) × size(Pj, 2) matrix that contracts the third index of the bracket tensor tensor with v²/2. With R = ReducedTensor(tensor, Pi, Pj) and (n₁, n₂) the grid indices of the node K,\n\nVelocityReducedMatrix[i, j] = ∑_K R[i, j, K] * v²[n₂] / 2 .\n\nv² holds the squared velocity nodes, dist.v .^ 2 of a GridDistribution: one entry per velocity node, in the order of dist.v. The result is view(prt, :, :, 1) of the PotentialReducedTensor prt whose one-column projection carries v²[n₂] / 2 in the row of each node.\n\nThe grid needs two dimensions, or the function throws an ArgumentError. v² needs nv entries, or it throws a DimensionMismatch, and the element type DT, or it throws an ArgumentError. PotentialReducedTensor gives the other checks, the node order, the tensor's contract and the allocations.\n\njulia> size(VelocityReducedMatrix(gt, ones(9, 2), ones(9, 1), [1.0, 4.0, 9.0]))   # gt as above\n(2, 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.VelocityReducedMatrix"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods._get_MC̃_cubic-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, AbstractVector, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"Home","text":"_get_MC̃_cubic(V, ∫dv, ∫vdv, ∫v²dv, v, ci, li, h₁, h₂)\n\nAssemble the cubic, four-index collision tensor MC̃[o, a, b, c] on the n₁ × n₂ phase-space grid, from the evaluation matrix V, the three moment matrices ∫dv, ∫vdv and ∫v²dv of the rectangle rule, the velocity grid v, the CartesianIndices ci and the LinearIndices li of the n₁ × n₂ grid, and the grid widths h₁ and h₂.\n\nv holds one entry per velocity node, so the call throws a DimensionMismatch under length(v) ≠ size(ci, 2). The tensor has the element type eltype(V).\n\nContracted with the grid values, Q₃[o] = Σ MC̃[o, a, b, c] f[a] f[b] f[c] is the Galerkin form of ρ²·C[f]. It conserves the discrete moments ∫dv·f, ∫vdv·f and ∫v²dv·f — the mass, the momentum and the energy — while f vanishes at the ends of the v-grid to eps(T). The v-stencil wraps the bounded grid with mod1, so a non-negligible end value breaks the conservation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods._get_MC̃_cubic"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods._get_MC̃_quadratic-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, AbstractVector, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"Home","text":"_get_MC̃_quadratic(V, ∫dv, ∫vdv, ∫v²dv, v, ci, li, h₁, h₂)\n\nAssemble the quadratic, three-index collision tensor MC̃[o, a, c] on the n₁ × n₂ phase-space grid, from the same arguments as _get_MC̃_cubic. v holds one entry per velocity node, so the call throws a DimensionMismatch under length(v) ≠ size(ci, 2), and the tensor has the element type eltype(V).\n\nContracted with the grid values, Q₂[o] = Σ MC̃[o, a, c] f[a] f[c] is the Galerkin form of ρ²·C[f] without the ρ̂u term. It conserves the mass ∫dv·f and the energy ∫v²dv·f, but not the momentum ∫vdv·f: it pulls the mean velocity toward v = 0, by a relative 1.3e-2 over ten RK4 steps of Δt = 10⁻³ on nx = 2, v = range(-10, 10; length = 25), from the shifted Maxwellian of the invariants testset in test/gridbased/collisions.jl. The moments are conserved only while f vanishes at the ends of the v-grid to eps(T).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods._get_MC̃_quadratic"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods._green_matrix-Tuple{GridDistribution, PoissonSolvers.Potential}","page":"Home","text":"_green_matrix(dist::GridDistribution, potential::Potential)\n\nThe n_x times n_x Green's matrix G of potential's backend on the grid of dist, which gives the field of a density in one product:\n\nrho_i = h_v sum_j f_ij  qquad phi = G rho \n\nIt solves the backend for the n_x unit vectors and symmetrises the result. The symmetry makes partial H  partial f = h_x h_v h exact, so the discrete Hamiltonian is quadratic in f and an implicit midpoint step conserves it. A spline basis is refused: its density at the nodes is not a Galerkin load vector. PoissonSolverMatrixFree bounds its residual, not its error, so in Float32 on a fine grid G is less accurate than eps(Float32). The errors are listed under VlasovPoisson.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods._green_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods._grid_1d1v_rhs!-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Home","text":"_grid_1d1v_rhs!(ḟ, t, f, params)\n\nArakawa's discretisation of the 1D1V Vlasov–Poisson right-hand side, as the vector field of a GeometricEquations.ODEProblem:\n\ndotf = - f h  qquad h_ij = fracv_j^22 + phi_i  qquad phi = G rho \nqquad rho_i = h_v sum_j f_ij \n\nfor a charge of +1, with G from _green_matrix. params holds the bracket arakawa, the velocity nodes v and G; the state f is vec(dist.values). The bracket is doubly periodic, so a distribution that reaches the ends of the velocity range wraps mass from v_b to v_a (see VlasovPoisson).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods._grid_1d1v_rhs!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods._snapshot_matrix-Tuple{AbstractArray{<:Any, 4}}","page":"Home","text":"The (n₁·n₂) × (n₃·n₄) reshape of Y; its column (p - 1) * n₃ + t is vec(Y[:, :, t, p]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods._snapshot_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.bracket_tensor-Union{Tuple{GeometricBrackets.Arakawa{DT}}, Tuple{DT}} where DT","page":"Home","text":"bracket_tensor(a::Arakawa{DT})\n\nThe rank-3 GridTensor of the bracket a on a's own nx × nv grid, with stencil width 1: its entry at the nodes (I, J, K) is a(I, J, K). The grid is read from a, so the tensor cannot be on another grid than its bracket. This is the tensor that PotentialReducedTensor, VelocityReducedMatrix and FullyReducedTensor take.\n\nbracket_tensor is a bridge until VlasovMethods requires GeometricBrackets 0.3, whose four-argument PoissonTensor(DT, nx, nv, f) builds the same tensor and checks the grid.\n\njulia> using GeometricBrackets: Arakawa\n\njulia> size(bracket_tensor(Arakawa(3, 4, 0.5, 0.5)))\n(12, 12, 12)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.bracket_tensor"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.check_conservation_basis-Tuple{SplineDistribution}","page":"Home","text":"check_conservation_basis(dist::SplineDistribution; moments = 2)\n\nThrow unless the basis reproduces every polynomial of degree ≤ moments, i.e. unless the moments int v^k f_s  dv for k ≤ moments agree with the particles'.\n\nNot called automatically: a diagnostic run may legitimately want a Dirichlet or periodic basis, and refusing to build one would be wrong. Call it where a result depends on the two representations agreeing — before reporting an entropy, or before comparing a spline moment against a particle one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.check_conservation_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.compute_J!-Union{Tuple{VD}, Tuple{XD}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, SplineDistribution{T, XD, VD}, Landau}} where {T, XD, VD}","page":"Home","text":"compute_J!(J, sdist, ::Landau)\n\nThe vector\n\nmathbbL_k = sum_i mathbbM^-1_ik int_Omega varphi_i(v)\n               left 1 + log Big( sum_j f_j varphi_j(v) Big) right dv\n\nof the Landau manuscript's eq:defn_Lk — the L^2 projection of 1 + log f_s onto the spline basis.\n\nWritten as a projection rather than as one quadrature per coefficient. The integrand is sampled once on the tensor-product Gauß-Legendre grid the basis already carries and contracted against the one-dimensional tabulations, so the cost is the grid size rather than the grid size times the number of coefficients. The earlier version called a gauss_quad_2d that looped over every cell pair for each of the M coefficients, and took the quadrature domain from a hard-coded knots[3:(end-1)] slice — asymmetric, independent of the spline order and of the boundary condition, and a different subrange from the one the mass matrix was assembled on, so the two halves of eq:defn_Lk were not computed over the same domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.compute_J!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.compute_J!-Union{Tuple{XD}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, SplineDistribution{T, XD, 1}, MetriplecticLenardBernstein}} where {T, XD}","page":"Home","text":"compute_J!(J, sdist, ::MetriplecticLenardBernstein)\n\nThe vector mathbbL_k = sum_i mathbbM^-1_ik int varphi_i  (1 + log f_s)  dv of the Landau manuscript's eq:defn_Lk, which is exactly the L^2 projection of 1 + log f_s onto the basis.\n\nThis discretisation projects 1 + log f_s onto the spline space and differentiates the projection, where eq:velocity_ode of the Lenard-Bernstein manuscript uses the pointwise ratio f_s(v_alpha)f_s(v_alpha). The two agree only up to the projection error of the logarithm. That is deliberate and is what makes this a genuine discrete-gradient system: J is partial S_h  partial f_i for S_h = int f_s log f_s  dv.\n\nwarning: The logarithm is unguarded in the manuscripts and guarded here\nThe earlier implementation wrote 0.5 * log(f_s^2). That is log|f_s| exactly, and its derivative is f_s'/f_s for either sign, so the drift it produces is finite and plausible-looking wherever f_s < 0. What it is not is the entropy: S = ∫ f log f requires f > 0, and where f_s < 0 the H-theorem reverses — dS/dt = -ν ∫ F²/f dv becomes positive. So the construction converted a positivity violation from a visible DomainError into an invisible wrong answer, and removed the only diagnostic that would have caught it. Positivity is checked here instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.compute_J!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.compute_K!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractArray{T}, Any, Landau}} where T","page":"Home","text":"compute_K!(K1, K2, v_array, sdist, landau)\n\nThe matrix mathbbK_kalpha = w_alpha  nabla varphi_k(v_alpha) of the Landau manuscript's appendix, split into its two velocity components.\n\nnote: Both matrices are cleared first\nThey are cache arrays, allocated once and written only at the entries whose basis functions the current particle positions overlap. Without the fill! the entries written on earlier calls survive: as particles move between cells, mathbbK accumulates spurious nonzeros from previous Picard iterations and previous time steps, and mathbbK^+ is then the pseudo-inverse of a matrix that is not w_alpha nabla varphi_k(v_alpha).\n\nEvery index is bounded per component before being flattened. Testing only the flat index does not bound the components: with i = 0, j = 3, M = 10 the flat index (j-1)M + i = 20 passes a 1 ≤ k ≤ M² test and decodes to (10, 2), so an out-of-support contribution is aliased onto an unrelated basis function instead of being discarded. Wrapping goes through basis_index, which is periodic-aware, so the gradient is now evaluated with the same wrap as the spline it differentiates — the two disagreed before.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.compute_K!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.compute_L!-Union{Tuple{XD}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, SplineDistribution{T, XD, 2}, Landau}} where {T, XD}","page":"Home","text":"compute_L!(L, sdist, landau; chunk = 256)\n\nThe symmetric matrix\n\nmathbbL_ij = frac12 int_Omega  int_Omega\n  big( nabla varphi_i(v) - nabla varphi_i(v) big) cdot\n  f_s(v)  U(vv)  f_s(v) cdot\n  big( nabla varphi_j(v) - nabla varphi_j(v) big)  dv  dv\n\nof the Landau manuscript's eq:discrete-landau-matrix.\n\nMethod\n\nExpanding the two differences gives four terms. With U symmetric in its arguments and in its indices, relabelling v leftrightarrow v shows the first and fourth are equal and the second and third are equal, so the factor of one half cancels and\n\nmathbbL = sum_cd D^c operatornamediag(s odot A^cd) (D^d)^mathsf T\n           - sum_cd big( D^c operatornamediag(s) big)  U^cd \n             big( D^d operatornamediag(s) big)^mathsf T \nqquad A^cd_a = sum_b s_b  U^cd_ab \n\nwith s_a = w_a f_s(v_a) and D^c the sparse gradient tables above. This is an identity, not an approximation: the same quadrature sum, rearranged.\n\nBoth terms are sparse-times-dense products. The first is a single pass over the grid; the second is the genuinely coupled one, and its Q times Q kernel is built and consumed in row blocks of chunk so that nothing of that size is ever held.\n\nU^cd is symmetric in (cd), so each block is swept once and its three independent components serve all four contractions. The whole assembly therefore costs O(Q^2) kernel evaluations, two sweeps of the grid pairs in total: one for A^cd and one for the blocks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.compute_L!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.compute_coefficients-Union{Tuple{VT}, Tuple{SplineDistribution, ParticleDistribution, AbstractArray{VT}}} where VT","page":"Home","text":"compute_coefficients(distribution, particle_dist, vp)\n\nThe coefficients A_1, A_2 of the conservative Lenard-Bernstein drift, from the requirement that the operator conserve momentum and energy:\n\nbeginpmatrix n_h  n_h u_h  n_h u_h  n_h varepsilon_h endpmatrix\nbeginpmatrix A_1  A_2 endpmatrix\n= - sum_alpha w_alpha beginpmatrix 1  v_alpha endpmatrix\n  fracf_s(v_alpha)f_s(v_alpha) \nqquad\nbeginaligned\nn_h = textstylesum_alpha w_alpha  \nn_h u_h = textstylesum_alpha w_alpha v_alpha  \nn_h varepsilon_h = textstylesum_alpha w_alpha v_alpha^2 \nendaligned\n\nsolved by Cramer's rule. This is eq:coefficient_lin_system of the Lenard-Bernstein manuscript.\n\nnote: The particle weights belong in every sum\nThey were absent from all five of them. Both the matrix and the right-hand side are linear in w, so for uniform weights the factor cancels and A_1 A_2 come out unchanged — which is why this never showed up: every initialiser in examples/ sets w = 1/npart. What the earlier version actually annihilated was sum_alpha dotv_alpha rather than sum_alpha w_alpha dotv_alpha, so with the non-uniform weights the importance-sampling initialiser produces, momentum and energy conservation were lost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.compute_coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.compute_coefficients_rclb-Union{Tuple{VT}, Tuple{SplineDistribution, ParticleDistribution, AbstractArray{VT}}} where VT","page":"Home","text":"compute_coefficients_rclb(distribution, particle_dist, vp)\n\nThe coefficients of the rescaled parametrisation, in which the coefficient of v is normalised to one:\n\ndotv_alpha = - nu left( A_1 fracf_s(v_alpha)f_s(v_alpha) + A_2 + v_alpha right) \n\nThis is the conservative operator with time rescaled by varepsilon_h - u_h^2, so that A_1^mathrmresc = 1  A_2^mathrmcons = varepsilon_h - u_h^2 and A_2^mathrmresc = A_1^mathrmcons  A_2^mathrmcons = -u_h. It is not the fixed-coefficient cumulant-scaling experiment of the manuscript appendix, which sets the coefficients by hand rather than solving for them.\n\nThe particle weights appear in every sum, for the reason given at compute_coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.compute_coefficients_rclb"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.compute_dS!-Union{Tuple{XD}, Tuple{ST}, Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractArray{ST}, SplineDistribution{ST, XD, 1}, MetriplecticLenardBernstein, ParticleDistribution}} where {ST, XD}","page":"Home","text":"compute_dS!(dS, J, v, sdist, ::MetriplecticLenardBernstein, pdist)\n\npartial S_h  partial v_alpha = w_alpha sum_k mathbbL_k  varphi_k(v_alpha), the Landau manuscript's eq:entropy_derivative up to its overall sign.\n\nThe weight w_alpha is read from pdist, which is why the particle distribution is passed in: it equals 1N for every initialiser in examples/ but not for the importance-sampling one, so 1/length(dS) is not a substitute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.compute_dS!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.compute_entropy-Tuple{Any, MetriplecticLenardBernstein}","page":"Home","text":"compute_entropy(f, mlb::MetriplecticLenardBernstein)\n\nS_h = int f_s log f_s  dv, the quantity the manuscript's entropy figures report.\n\nIntegrated on the Gauß-Legendre grid of the basis rather than adaptively, so that it is the same quadrature the discretisation itself uses. Returns the value alone; the earlier version returned a quadgk error estimate alongside it and wrote 0.5*log(f^2), so it reported int f logf rather than the entropy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.compute_entropy"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.draw!-Tuple{ParticleDistribution{1, 1, VD, PT} where {VD, PT<:(ParticleMethods.ParticleList{1, ST} where ST<:AbstractMatrix{1})}, Union{Function, Type}, Bump, VlasovMethods.AcceptRejectSampling}","page":"Home","text":"Returns particles drawn from g for the bump-on-tail case.\n\ninput: nb. pf particles Nₚ, x-marginal function gₓ, parameters μ output: Particles struct\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.draw!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.draw!-Tuple{ParticleDistribution{1, 1, VD, PT} where {VD, PT<:(ParticleMethods.ParticleList{1, ST} where ST<:AbstractMatrix{1})}, Union{Function, Type}, BumpOnTail, VlasovMethods.AcceptRejectSampling}","page":"Home","text":"Returns particles drawn from g for the bump-on-tail case.\n\ninput: nb. pf particles Nₚ, x-marginal function gₓ, parameters μ output: Particles struct\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.draw!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.draw!-Tuple{ParticleDistribution{1, 1, VD, PT} where {VD, PT<:(ParticleMethods.ParticleList{1, ST} where ST<:AbstractMatrix{1})}, Union{Function, Type}, BumpOnTail, VlasovMethods.ImportanceSampling}","page":"Home","text":"Returns particles drawn from g for the bump-on-tail case.\n\ninput: nb. pf particles Nₚ, x-marginal function gₓ, parameters μ output: Particles struct\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.draw!"},{"category":"function","location":"#VlasovMethods.electric_field","page":"Home","text":"electric_field(field, t, x)\nmagnetic_field(field, t, x)\n\nThe electric and magnetic field of field at the time t and the position x, each an SVector{3,T} in Cartesian components, with T the element type of x.\n\nThe two functions are the interface between a VlasovWithExternalFields model and the field its particles move in. The model calls them once per particle and per kick, with x the particle's position and t the time the kick starts at, and a field type defines one method of each. There is no abstract field type and no fallback method, so a field that defines neither raises its MethodError at the first kick inside integrate.\n\nUniformField is the one field type in this package. The ElectromagneticFields extension adds methods for that package's FieldFunctions of a Cartesian chart; see VlasovWithExternalFields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.electric_field"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.field_basis-Tuple{Union{ReducedBasisMethods.PODEVD, ReducedBasisMethods.PODSVD}, Snapshots}","page":"Home","text":"field_basis(algorithm::Union{PODSVD, PODEVD}, s::Snapshots; parameters = NamedTuple(),\n            kwargs...)\n\nThe POD ReducedBasis of the snapshot matrix of the accelerations s.A, whose nd * nₚ rows match the position block of particle_basis. algorithm is the tag PODSVD or PODEVD of ReducedBasisMethods, which VlasovMethods does not export. A PSD or complex tag would split the rows of A into positions and velocities, so it throws a MethodError.\n\nparameters, s.paramspace and kwargs, k or tolerance, go to ReducedBasis unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.field_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.gradient_tabulations-Union{Tuple{SplineDistribution{T, XD, 2}}, Tuple{XD}, Tuple{T}} where {T, XD}","page":"Home","text":"gradient_tabulations(sdist)\n\nThe sparse tables D^c_ka = partial_c varphi_k(v_a) of the two velocity components of the basis gradient at every point of the tensor-product quadrature grid, as an M × Q pair.\n\nBuilt as Kronecker products of the one-dimensional tabulations the quadrature already holds: partial_1 (varphi_i otimes varphi_j) = varphi_i otimes varphi_j, so D¹ = kron(Φ₂, Φ₁') and D² = kron(Φ₂', Φ₁) — with the row and column orderings that LinearIndices and the grid enumeration already use. Nothing is evaluated that the quadrature had not already tabulated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.gradient_tabulations"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.kernel-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, AbstractVector{T}, Landau}} where T","page":"Home","text":"kernel(v_α, v_β, ::Landau)\n\nThe Landau collision kernel\n\nU(u) = frac1u left( mathbbI - fracu otimes uu^2 right) \nqquad u = v_alpha - v_beta \n\nthe orthogonal projector onto the complement of u, scaled by 1u.\n\nThis is the standard Landau tensor and it is not what the manuscript's eq:landau_kernel as typeset says: that carries a prefactor 1u^3 in front of the bracketed projector, i.e. an extra u^-2. The intended reading is u^-3 ( u^2 mathbbI - u otimes u ), which is this. The properties the conservation proofs rest on hold for the expression implemented here: U is symmetric, U(v_alpha v_beta) = U(v_beta v_alpha), and U u = 0 exactly.\n\nwarning: The coincident-point value is a regularisation, not a limit\nU diverges as u to 0; there is no value to return there. Zero is returned so that a product quadrature whose two factors share nodes does not produce Inf, but the error this introduces does not vanish under refinement — and because both factors of the double integral in compute_L! use the same Gauß-Legendre nodes, it fires on every diagonal cell pair rather than on a set of measure zero. The kernel is integrable in two dimensions, so the integral itself is finite; what is needed is a quadrature that knows about the singularity, or offset grids for the two factors. Neither is implemented.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"#VlasovMethods.magnetic_field","page":"Home","text":"magnetic_field(field, t, x)\n\nThe magnetic field of field at the time t and the position x. See electric_field for the interface the two functions share.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.magnetic_field"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.particle_basis-Tuple{Any, Snapshots}","page":"Home","text":"particle_basis(algorithm, s::Snapshots; parameters = NamedTuple(), kwargs...)\n\nThe ReducedBasis of the stacked [Q; P] snapshot matrix of s: the nd * nₚ rows of the positions X above the nd * nₚ rows of the velocities V, one column per saved time and sample. algorithm is one of the tags PODSVD, PODEVD, CotangentLiftSVD, CotangentLiftEVD and ComplexSVD of ReducedBasisMethods, which VlasovMethods does not export.\n\nparameters, s.paramspace and kwargs, k or tolerance, go to ReducedBasis unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.particle_basis"},{"category":"function","location":"#VlasovMethods.plot_distribution","page":"Home","text":"plot_distribution(dist) -> Figure\nplot_distribution(dist; xdomain, vdomain, nx = 100, nv = 100) -> Figure\n\nA phase-space portrait of dist on one axis, x horizontally and v vertically.\n\nDefined in a package extension: load CairoMakie to make it available.\n\nusing VlasovMethods, CairoMakie\nfig = plot_distribution(dist)\n\nThe one-argument method takes a GridDistribution and draws dist.values as a single heatmap on the grid's own nodes, untransposed: the columns of the matrix are the v nodes, so the figure and the matrix have the same orientation.\n\nThe method with the two keywords takes a ParticleDistribution of one position and one velocity dimension and draws the weighted particle histogram on nx × nv cells of xdomain and vdomain. Cell (i, j) holds\n\nF_ij = frac1h_x h_v sum_k w_k \n\nsummed over the particles that fall in it, with h_x = (x_b - x_a)  n_x and h_v = (v_b - v_a)  n_v, so the histogram integrates to the total weight of the particles it keeps. A cell is half-open, [x_a + (i-1) h_x, x_a + i h_x) and likewise in v.\n\nxdomain is periodic and x wraps into it. vdomain is bounded, and a particle outside it — v < v_a or v >= v_b — is dropped from the histogram rather than clamped into an end cell. A particle with a non-finite x, which the wrap cannot place, is dropped as well. A distribution with no particles throws an ArgumentError.\n\nThere are no other keywords, and the two domains are required. Use the returned Figure to set a title, a size or an axis label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.plot_distribution"},{"category":"function","location":"#VlasovMethods.plot_particles","page":"Home","text":"plot_particles(dist; xdomain, vdomain) -> Figure\n\nThe particles of a ParticleDistribution of one position and one velocity dimension, as a single scatter of (x, v).\n\nDefined in a package extension: load CairoMakie to make it available.\n\nusing VlasovMethods, CairoMakie\nfig = plot_particles(dist; xdomain = (0.0, 2π), vdomain = (-4.0, 4.0))\n\nxdomain is periodic and x wraps into it. Every particle's velocity is plotted as it is, and the axis limits are set to xdomain horizontally and vdomain vertically, so a particle at v outside vdomain is left at that v and lies outside the axes. A distribution with no particles throws an ArgumentError.\n\nThere are no other keywords, and the two domains are required. Use the returned Figure to set a title, a size or an axis label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.plot_particles"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.project_Maxwellian-Union{Tuple{SplineDistribution{T, XD, VD}}, Tuple{VD}, Tuple{XD}, Tuple{T}} where {T, XD, VD}","page":"Home","text":"project_Maxwellian(sdist::SplineDistribution)\n\nProject the normalised Maxwellian onto the basis of sdist.\n\nThe projection goes through the Gauß-Legendre quadrature the basis already carries, in any number of velocity dimensions. project_function calls f with a scalar when VD == 1 and with a VD-tuple otherwise, and SVector accepts both, so one method covers every dimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.project_Maxwellian"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.project_function-Tuple{Any, SplineDistribution}","page":"Home","text":"project_function(f, sdist::SplineDistribution)\n\nThe L^2 projection of the function f onto the basis of sdist, written into its coefficients.\n\nf is called with a scalar for a one-dimensional velocity space and with a VD-tuple otherwise. This is eq:numerical-example-1-coefficients of the Lenard-Bernstein manuscript, hatf = mathbbM^-1 int f varphi_i  dv, and it works in any number of velocity dimensions rather than only in two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.project_function"},{"category":"function","location":"#VlasovMethods.projection!","page":"Home","text":"\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.projection!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.projection!-Tuple{PoissonSolvers.Potential, ParticleDistribution}","page":"Home","text":"projection!(potential::PoissonSolvers.Potential, distribution::ParticleDistribution)\n\nDeposit the particle weights of distribution onto the basis of potential, writing the charge density into the potential's right-hand side buffer.\n\nEach particle contributes to the local_width(basis) basis functions that do not vanish at its position. evaluate_all! reduces a periodic argument into the domain and writes those values into a buffer, returning the index of the first; basis_index is what wraps it, and is the identity where the basis is not periodic. The particle state keeps the unwrapped position, so the reduction belongs to the evaluation.\n\nA recombined basis has no single width: a function near an end spans the union of two parent supports, so local_width is the largest block and a cell with fewer nonzero functions leaves the tail of the buffer zero. Those padding entries carry indices past nbasis(basis), which is why the zeros are skipped rather than added.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.projection!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.projection-Union{Tuple{XD}, Tuple{ST}, Tuple{PT}, Tuple{VT}, Tuple{AbstractVector{VT}, ParticleDistribution{PT, XD, 1, PT1} where PT1<:(ParticleMethods.ParticleList{PT, ST} where ST<:AbstractMatrix{PT}), SplineDistribution{ST, XD, 1}}} where {VT, PT, ST, XD}","page":"Home","text":"projection(velocities, dist::ParticleDistribution, final_dist::SplineDistribution)\n\nProject the particles onto the basis of final_dist, writing the coefficients into it and returning its Spline.\n\nvelocities is a vector for a one-dimensional velocity space and a VD × N matrix otherwise; it is passed separately from dist because the implicit solvers evaluate the right-hand side at a stage value rather than at the particles' stored positions.\n\nwarning: A particle outside the domain is an error, not a warning\nThe velocity domain is truncated and the true one is not, so a particle can leave. When it does there is nothing sensible to do with it: it contributes nothing to the projection, so its weight is lost from f_s while it keeps moving, and f_s then evaluates to zero at its position — which makes the f_sf_s of every collision operator here a division by zero. The Lenard-Bernstein manuscript says as much in its closing remarks.A DomainError naming the count and the domain is therefore the only useful response: a run that has lost mass is not a run whose output means anything. Enlarge the domain, or give the mesh oversized end cells with length_big_cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.projection"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.rhs!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST}, AbstractArray{ST}, ParticleDistribution, Any, Any, Any, AbstractArray{ST}, Any, Any, MetriplecticLenardBernstein}} where ST","page":"Home","text":"(varepsilon_h - u_h^2)  mathbbL  partial S_h  partial v, i.e. the bracket of rhs_downstairs_factor! with its denominator cleared.\n\nUseful where the common factor is supplied elsewhere; it is not dotv, which is what rhs_downstairs_factor! returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.rhs!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.rhs_downstairs_factor!-Union{Tuple{ST}, Tuple{AbstractArray{ST}, AbstractArray{ST}, ParticleDistribution, Any, Any, Any, AbstractArray{ST}, Any, Any, MetriplecticLenardBernstein}} where ST","page":"Home","text":"The metriplectic Lenard-Bernstein right-hand side, dotv = mathbbL  partial S_h  partial v with the bracket\n\nmathbbL_alphabeta = - fracn_hw_alpha  delta_alphabeta\n  + frac(varepsilon_h - u_h v_alpha) + (v_alpha - u_h) v_betavarepsilon_h - u_h^2 \n\nmathbbL is symmetric and satisfies sum_alpha w_alpha mathbbL_alphabeta = 0 and sum_alpha w_alpha v_alpha mathbbL_alphabeta = 0, so momentum and energy are exact Casimirs of the bracket whatever dS is — a stronger structure than the manuscript's, and the reason these runs conserve.\n\nThe weight is per particle, read from pdist for each alpha: the two degeneracies above are what fail if a single weight is used for every particle, so with non-uniform weights the conservation the bracket is built for would be lost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.rhs_downstairs_factor!"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.sodeensemble-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{VlasovPoisson{1, 1, <:ParticleDistribution{T, XD, VD, PT} where {XD, VD, PT<:(ParticleMethods.ParticleList{T, ST} where ST<:AbstractMatrix{T})}}, AbstractVector{<:ParticleDistribution{T, 1, 1, PT} where PT<:(ParticleMethods.ParticleList{T, ST} where ST<:AbstractMatrix{T})}, Tuple, Real}} where T","page":"Home","text":"sodeensemble(model, members, tspan, tstep)\n\nThe training data of model as a GeometricEquations.SODEEnsemble, one sample per ParticleDistribution in members.\n\nThe initial condition of a member m is (q = copy(m.particles.z),), and its parameters are its own weights, (w = copy(m.particles.w),), because an importance-sampled draw gives each member different weights. Both are copies, so a later change to m does not reach the problem. The invariant h is the VlasovHamiltonian of model.\n\nGeometricIntegrators.integrate(ens, Strang()) solves every member into one EnsembleSolution, which h5save and h5load round-trip through the HDF5 extension of GeometricSolutions. The invariant is evaluated along a stored solution once per saved state, the initial state included, with\n\ncompute_invariant(sol.t, sol[i][:q], parameters(ens)[i], invariants(equation(ens)).h)\n\nintegrate itself evaluates no invariant.\n\nAll members share the potential and the work buffer of model. Solve them one after another, as integrate does: members solved concurrently read each other's fields, with no error.\n\nAn empty member list, and a member whose weights and state have different particle counts, throw an ArgumentError. The model and the members have one element type T; a member of another element type raises a MethodError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.sodeensemble"},{"category":"method","location":"#VlasovMethods.velocity_moments-Union{Tuple{GridDistribution{DT, XT, VT, FT} where {XT<:AbstractRange{DT}, VT<:AbstractRange{DT}, FT<:AbstractMatrix{DT}}}, Tuple{DT}} where DT","page":"Home","text":"velocity_moments(dist::GridDistribution)\n\nThe velocity moments of dist at each x-node, as the named tuple (density, momentum, energy) of three vectors of length nx:\n\nn_i = sum_j f_ij h_v  qquad\np_i = sum_j v_j f_ij h_v  qquad\nvarepsilon_i = frac12 sum_j v_j^2 f_ij h_v \n\nenergy is the kinetic energy density, with the factor tfrac12. The sums are the rectangle rule of _apply_∫dv! and its moment stencils.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VlasovMethods.velocity_moments"}]
}
